Emacspeak News --History Of User-Visible Changes

Author: T. V. Raman <tv.raman.tv@gmail.com>

* Emacspeak 61 --- Emacspeak 61 User Visible Changes

This version requires Emacs-29.1 or later.

** Native Windows Speech Under WSL

Emacsvox now owns its native Windows speech stack.  The bundled
~windows-outloud~ and ~windows-dtk~ servers provide Eloquence and Software
DECtalk through native Windows bridges, while ~windows-play~ provides ordered,
interruptible auditory icons.  Main and notification speech can use separate
native streams with configurable stereo positions.

Use ~make windows-speech~ to build the complete stack, or the
~windows-audio~, ~windows-outloud~, and ~windows-dtk~ targets separately.
Select a server with ~M-x tts-select-server~, configure native auditory icons
with ~emacsvox-windows-speech-configure-audio~, and inspect dependencies with
~emacsvox-windows-speech-diagnose~.  Proprietary Eloquence files and the
separately licensed DECtalk runtime are not stored in the repository.
Replaceable navigation transactions are discarded when newer input is
waiting, and adjacent formatting runs are now batched into one native
utterance per auditory-icon-delimited segment.  This prevents rapid movement
from leaving obsolete synthesis work queued after the user stops.

** Omnivox Logical Voice Discovery And Registration

The Omnivox adapter now negotiates its bounded, versioned control channel and
discovers structured engine, capability, and physical-voice inventory without
injecting server events into speech.  It registers Emacsvox logical voices and
normalized ACSS styles atomically with both main and notification processes.

Customize ~omnivox-logical-voice-preferences~ to give a logical voice ordered
exact, engine-default, or property selectors.  Exact selectors retain separate
engine and native voice IDs; property selectors resolve against each machine's
actual inventory.  Missing engines and voices degrade through configured
alternatives, and unresolved definitions remain visible in
~omnivox-logical-voice-registration~.  Run
~omnivox-register-logical-voices~ after changing preferences at runtime.

Registration does not yet route speech between engines.  The existing speech
path remains compatible while Omnivox's engine registry and per-span routing
are implemented.

** TTS Prefix Map Rename

The generic speech prefix map is now ~emacsvox-tts-submap~.  The former
~emacsvox-dtk-submap~ name has been removed without a compatibility alias.
User configuration that binds or inspects that map must use the new name.
The default _C-e d_ key sequence is unchanged.

** Aural Presentation Schemes

Emacsvox now has semantic aural presentation schemes.  A scheme can combine
speech, device-independent voices, auditory cues, pauses, and portable stereo
placement before or after content.  The default scheme preserves existing
voices and icons; selectable Org examples demonstrate alternative
presentations.

Use ~emacsvox-list-aural-schemes~, ~emacsvox-set-aural-scheme~, and
~emacsvox-edit-aural-scheme~ to inspect, select, and customize schemes.  Rules
can apply globally or by module, major or derived mode, session, or buffer.
The scheme list is now a spoken manager for viewing direct and inherited
presentations, editing, copying, deleting, renaming, activating, previewing,
and validating schemes.  Built-ins remain read-only; deletion protects
schemes that are inherited by others, and rename updates child schemes.
Row and column movement speaks the column title and value, and announces the
top, bottom, first-column, and last-column boundaries.
The semantic vocabulary list now provides the same titled-cell and boundary
navigation.  Preferred interactive command aliases share the discoverable
~emacsvox-aural-~ prefix; established verb-first names remain compatible.
One base scheme remains active at a time, with any number of independently
enabled presentation options layered over it in explicit order.  These are
the user-facing form of optional feature fragments: they can add speech, cues,
or styling without copying or replacing the base scheme, while persistent
personal rules remain stronger.
Use ~emacsvox-aural-list-feature-fragments~ for the spoken presentation-option
manager.  Its normal view groups options into expandable integration
collections; press ~a~ for enabled options in exact weakest-to-strongest
order.  Toggling preserves an option's stable precedence.  The manager
supports viewing, creating, copying, editing, deleting, validating, toggling,
and explicitly reordering options; all changes save atomically.
Press ~P~ to preview an option without changing its enabled state; the normal
preview composes it with the active configuration, while ~C-u P~ plays the
option alone.  A matching object in the remembered source buffer is previewed
with its live facts and mode.  Otherwise the manager clearly identifies a
simulation and offers representative examples supplied by the integration,
with safe selector-derived examples for rules the provider did not cover.
Org supplies heading-level and folded-heading examples.  Notmuch supplies
unread-result and attachment examples for the shared mail-status option.
Data-only integration definitions now use the
~emacsvox-aural-provider-SCOPE.el~ naming convention.  Org, Markdown,
Notmuch, and shared workflow providers are available at startup without
loading their external packages; the corresponding ~emacsvox-SCOPE.el~
libraries retain live hooks, fact capture, advice, and speech commands.
The former internal libraries ~emacsvox-aural-org~,
~emacsvox-aural-markdown~, and ~emacsvox-aural-representative~ have been
replaced by the provider names without compatibility aliases.
Optional built-in Org options independently add a heading-level label, a
section cue, or folded/opened feedback.  Speech actions may safely substitute
registered semantic values with templates such as ~Heading {level}~; every
field must be guaranteed by the selector, and Emacs converts the result to
literal text before queueing it.  Org headings now always expose the
registered ~visibility~ attribute as ~folded~ or ~expanded~, while preserving
the older folded state for compatible rules.
The default scheme editor is now a spoken form: TAB and Shift-TAB move through
natural-language fields, RET edits one field, and built-in schemes can be
copied from inside the workflow.  Use
~emacsvox-edit-aural-scheme-advanced~ for the complete declarative rule view.
~emacsvox-explain-aural-presentation~ reports the facts and winning rules at
point.  It infers an occasion with matching rules, speaks the resolved
before/content/after order, and accepts a prefix argument to choose an
occasion explicitly.  Existing icon calls, personality properties, face
mappings, and sound directories remain supported through compatibility
adapters.
Personal rules can now match a named visual face with the ~:legacy-face~
selector and combine an appended or prepended cue with a palette voice.
Ordered face lists remain layered: all matching rules may contribute actions,
the strongest face breaks otherwise tied scalar voice settings, and semantic
identity remains preferred within the same origin.  The spoken scheme editor
can create and edit face presentations directly, and explanation reports the
captured faces strongest first.
Interactive Up and Down line movement now submits the same ~navigation~ and
~focus-entered~ semantics as structural movement commands.  Arriving on an
Org heading with an arrow key therefore uses the same heading presentation as
~org-next-visible-heading~; continuous-reading commands remain ~continuous~.
Notmuch search buffers explicitly keep Up and Down equivalent to ~C-n~ and
~C-p~; the existing ~n~ and ~p~ thread-navigation commands are unchanged.
Use ~emacsvox-aural-list-profiles~ to save and switch complete named
combinations of a base scheme, ordered feature fragments, sound pack, voice
palette, and portable spatial settings.  Profiles reference existing
components instead of copying their rules, and activation is transactional.
Exactly one persisted profile identity is selected.  It is shown as ~active~
while live settings match its saved values and as ~modified~ after the scheme,
presentation-option order, sound, voice, or spatial settings change.  Other
profiles remain inactive even when they happen to contain identical values.
Personal data schema version 6 adds this selected identity; older files migrate
automatically.
Use ~emacsvox-aural-doctor~ for a spoken report of live bindings, loaded source
or byte-code, active presentation, resources, personal data, spatial fallback,
and speech-server state.  Repairs are explicit and limited to deterministic
safe operations.
Notmuch, Gnus, Dired, and Magit now submit registered workflow facts around
their existing speech and auditory cues.  Schemes and optional fragments can
therefore distinguish mail status, filesystem state, and version-control
sections without changing the default feedback.
Their remaining legacy cue paths are now fully enclosed by semantic
presentation boundaries, including message parts, attachments, thread
boundaries, group operations, filesystem inspection, Magit views, blame,
diffs, and process completion.
Agent Shell is now an end-to-end reference integration for transcript
content, session lifecycle, tools, permissions, source blocks, tables,
viewports, and prompt editing.  Markdown now exposes headings, tasks, lists,
links, code blocks, tables, footnotes, visibility, and document operations,
and line movement uses the same semantic heading intent as structural
navigation.  A source audit maintains the migrated-module set and rejects new
context-free direct icon calls while allowing named compatibility adapters.

** Search From Calendar

You can perform before/after  Web and Youtube searches from within the
Emacs Calendar. Keys: _gg_, _gG_, _gy_ and _gY_ in Calendar.

** EMPV And Youtube

More empv-aware commands.


** Speech-enable Consult

Module ~emacspeak-consult~ speech-enables the ~consult~ package.
